eing able then to have him  reserve  the opportunity to to to send extra uh  sample into areas where we start to see  the beginnings of an outbreak it's very  interesting to hear about the  focus on regional uh testing regional  uh sampling and do you think we need to  scale up testing across the united  kingdom really to get a bit more of a  grip about  where these outbreaks are then well i  think um  you know under the leadership of  baroness harding and there's been a big  move and certainly we have been helping  the joint biosecurity center to  to get really fast uh data not just  from testing but also for example from  measuring  uh the extent of the virus in wastewater  due to get early  early sites of any particular outbreak  and then you're absolutely right  there has to be very large uh testing  uh in those areas uh with tracing  to to be able to to keep the um  the epidemic under control uh and  certainly i think that's where that  uh if you like rise in the uh ability  of us to test and the number of tests is  incredibly important  yeah really important particularly of  course as the lockdown does continue to  be eased  um you were saying a little earlier that  things have been quite flat on the  number of infections  would you have expected uh to see  an increase in the number of infections  as lockdown was eased if it was going to  if you like lead to it i think it's a  really good  question sophie uh if i may and of  course  the answer really does depend on how the  population  works because you know the message has  been quite consistent and that if we  must  be alert and we must socially distance  and  if we are really super careful and if we  are able to  um follow all the rules it does seem to  me  that we should expect there to  be a relative um flatline  at the moment clearly um over the autumn  uh we will need to be ever vigilant  yeah certainly a really important thing  to go for  going forward i just want to talk to you  a little bit now about  the numbers and the data because it is  obviously  we're told by the government that the  government is using data to drive  the response to coronavirus to influence  policy and yet there's been a lot of  questions this week  about whether the data can be trusted  the health secretary  matt hancock uh are ordering this urgent  review into the way public health  england  counts coronavare's deaths in england  specifically uh saying that if someone  was previously diagnosed with covert 19  but subsequently died of unrelated  causes  they would still be classed as a  coronavirus death in that england  daily tally i mean this feels quite  astonishing to me  are these figures fit for purpose uh  well i think  i think it's really really important to  recognize that  different statistics are used for  different things  uh and so the phu data  are basically aimed to look at if you  like trends  over time in in in recent times if you  want to know  the accurate data on deaths then my  because i would say this but my  recommendation is that you go to the ons  data  why should you go to the ons data well  we believe very firmly  as does everyone that the very best  person  to take a decision uh around  what is the cause of death is the  medical practitioner who is there  at the time let's remember sadly um this  is  a loss every death or loss to to family  and to  to to friends and the medical  practitioner takes that view that's why  we are part of a who referral  group which very early in the pandemic  works out how to if you like make  covid19  a cause of death and then we worked with  our colleagues  at national records in scotland to to  get how to define that  into the advice that goes to medical  practitioners therefore  our data published weekly by  uh has by the medical practitioner a  decision on what  was the cause of death and i really  enjoyed your article by the way  that it was yesterday the day before but  i would say  that your point someone who had coveted  and then gets run over by a bus i think  it is  you know virtually it's inconceivable  but a medical practitioner  uh looking at a road accident he's going  to say that was the core the cause  was curving 19. therefore um my  recommendation  go with the ons data um we're a we  we've worked very hard to make our data  very quick  uh and probably um well it's certainly  among the fastest uh recorded uh  data by cause of death anywhere in the  world i mean  you're right of course the ons is the  gold standard of data and that different  statistics are used in different ways  but you can't really blame us for  focusing on the daily death data when  that was the one that the government was  talking about in its daily press  conferences  it feels to me like you're saying we  can't really trust it  i know that's true what i'm saying very  clearly is that we can trust  it to tell you whether there is a trend  in the last few days  or in the previous days that's exactly  what it was put together for  and that's exactly why it's there that's  why we we acknowledge you know for  example there are  always reductions in those later at the  weekend because  there are no registrations uh on that so  they so it allows us to look  at the recent trend but if you're  interested in the really accurate data  you you don't have to wait long let's be  honest stick with the ons data  okay uh the email on sunday reports  today that they think that the death  toll in england according to these phd  numbers could have been overestimated by  around four thousand people  do you think that's right well um  i need to to look very carefully um at  those data i mean i still stick very  much  with our data which uh are showing um  that around about forty eight thousand  one hundred fifty four people at the  moment have  been reported as dying due to covet and  those are the data that i would stick  with  okay now it's not just the phe data that  there's been a question mark over  it too sky news investigation found that  at the height of the pandemic  the government overstated the number of  people who had been tested for  coronavirus  by 200 000. we were told two million  people have been tested by mid-may  that was then revised down to 1.8  million i mean the statistics we have on  coronavirus  are dictating the policy the data is so  important when it comes to public  confidence how have we gotten such a  mess over numbers  well because it's great to have data  journalists such as ed conway working  with you  uh sophie as i may say so and look  you know i think early on in those  testing statistics  we were putting something together new  uh as a country  and i think there were some challenges  and i have to say when s has worked  hard with dhsc to  get into a position where we can trust  those data and the letter last week from  the office of statistics regulation  that's the statistics regulator  um complementing um the progress that  had been made  i think is an important moment okay now  the uk was also quite slow to  recommend the uh use of face masks face  coverings uh to stop the transmission of  the virus i know you've been doing a bit  of work  on whether or not people are wearing  them now uh what what  is it that you've been finding sure um  well  we have also had a weekly survey which  has gone out to a  some a representative sample of the  population right across the country  uh and we analyze those data incredibly  quickly and what we're showing  is that we come week there is an  increase in the number of people who are  using face covering so last week the  data published  uh just four or five days ago uh would  show that 61 of the population  had used um face covering in the last  week  and that's up from 50 uh the week before  um in in scotland 77 percent of the  population using  it for shopping and that that i think  will continue to go up as it's become  mandatory um in england 40 but of course  as that becomes mandatory next saturday  uh we will see that increase as well  but certainly uh people using uh face  covering and becoming it becoming  the norm in many uh parts of society  very interesting to see uh that kind of  significant change uh in such a quick  period of time and just finally i just  want to talk about the economic hit  of coronavares because there was hope  that we would  bounce back quite quickly once lockdown  was over but i have to say if you look  at the latest gdp data  it shows the economy only grew 1.8  in may now that of course is much lower  than the 5.5 percent expected  is the idea of a v-shaped recovery for  the birds  certainly not um but equally  it's too early to tell certainly that  one point eight percent  which is 24.5 percent lower than  february  was uh lower than uh many analysts  were expecting and hoping for uh and  um we will be looking over the next  little while  to see the extent to which um  it increases certainly we did see some  some indication with manufacturing up  eight percent of construction up eight  percent  of a return but clearly there is much to  do  in places like hospitality uh and  in accommodation i would say again that  our data  are showing that week on week people  are more saying they are more likely  to to go to a restaurant for example  and so we will i think see the economy  pick up but how fast it will pick up  with that v-shaped recovery that you  referred to sophie  it's too early to tell really  interesting to talk uh city and diamond  thank you very much for being on the  program this morning thank you  it's been my pleasure thank you

